D = "ivx_phenomenology"
ITEMS = [
dict(d=D, c="ivx jaspers primary delusion", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="Jaspers distinguished “primary” (true) delusions from “delusion-like ideas.” What, for Jaspers, is the decisive criterion?",
a="Primary delusions cannot be understood as arising from the person’s prior experiences, mood or personality; delusion-like ideas can",
ae="Jaspers’ three external features (extraordinary conviction, imperviousness to counterargument, impossible content) do not separate the two. The key is genetic understandability (verstehen): can the belief be traced empathically to earlier experiences or affect? A depressed patient’s belief that she has ruined her family is understandable from her mood; a sudden certainty that a traffic light means she is chosen is not.",
w=[("Primary delusions are always bizarre in content; delusion-like ideas are always plausible",
"Bizarre content is neither necessary nor sufficient. A primary delusion can have mundane content (a spouse’s infidelity), and a delusion-like idea can be strange."),
("Primary delusions are held with absolute certainty; delusion-like ideas with partial doubt",
"Degree of certainty is one of the external features Jaspers considered insufficient. Both may be held with full conviction."),
("Primary delusions arise in organic disease; delusion-like ideas arise in functional psychosis",
"Jaspers’ distinction is phenomenological and psychological, not etiological.")],
x="Jaspers, in General Psychopathology (1913), insisted that the false-belief definition misses the essence. The primary delusional experience is a transformation of meaning. Modern phenomenologists (Sass, Parnas, Fuchs) build on this: delusion as a changed way of being in the world, not merely a wrong proposition. Interview technique follows: ask how the belief arose, what the moment of realization was like, and whether it emerged from understandable feelings.",
s="Jaspers, General Psychopathology (1913)",
h="Jaspers’ test is not “Is it false?” but “Can I follow how they got there?” If the path is invisible, it’s primary."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx delusional perception two membered", m="ask", l=3, hard=True,
q="A patient says, “I saw the red car parked outside and I knew immediately that I had been chosen to start a new religion.” Which phenomenon is this, and how is it structured?",
a="Delusional perception: a normal perception followed by an abnormal meaning, with no understandable link between them",
ae="Kurt Schneider described delusional perception as two-membered: the perception itself is normal (the red car really is there), and an abnormal, self-referential significance is attached to it without any comprehensible connection. It is one of Schneider’s first-rank symptoms.",
w=[("A visual hallucination with secondary delusional elaboration",
"The car is real, so this is not a hallucination. The abnormality lies entirely in the meaning attached to the perception."),
("A delusion of reference arising from depressive guilt",
"Ideas of reference can be understandable in mood disorders. Here there is no understandable link between red car and religious mission."),
("An autochthonous delusion, which arises from a perception",
"Autochthonous delusions arise out of the blue, without a triggering perception. That is the key difference from delusional perception.")],
x="Interview tip: when a patient reports a sudden realization, ask, “What were you doing at that moment? Was there something you saw or heard?” This separates delusional perception (perception-triggered) from autochthonous delusion (sudden idea) and from delusional mood (a vague sense that something is about to happen). Schneider’s first-rank symptoms have limited specificity for schizophrenia, but their phenomenology still sharpens descriptions.",
s="Schneider, Clinical Psychopathology (1959); Sims, Symptoms in the Mind",
h="Delusional perception = 2 members: “Real thing + Unreal meaning.” Red car → religion."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx delusional mood", m="ask", l=3, hard=True,
q="A 19-year-old says everything has felt strange for weeks: the street looks the same but “different,” as though something important is about to happen, and it frightens him. What is the best term, and what follows clinically?",
a="Delusional mood (Wahnstimmung); watch closely for crystallization of a specific delusion",
ae="Delusional mood is a sense that the familiar world has changed, often uncanny, threatening or ominous, without a specific delusional idea yet. It frequently precedes the formation of a delusion, which can bring relief by giving the dread a content. It is an important prodromal feature.",
w=[("Depersonalization, indicating a dissociative rather than psychotic process",
"Depersonalization concerns feeling detached from the self. Delusional mood concerns a sense that the world has changed meaning, with a feeling of foreboding."),
("Generalized anxiety, which requires reassurance and sleep hygiene",
"Treating this as worry misses its quality: the change in the appearance and meaning of the world."),
("Déjà vu, a benign phenomenon common in young adults",
"Déjà vu is a brief, false sense of familiarity. Delusional mood is sustained and involves strangeness and significance.")],
x="Klaus Conrad, in Die beginnende Schizophrenie (1958), described stages: trema (tension and foreboding, overlapping with delusional mood), apophany (the revelation of meaning), anastrophe (a turning back onto the self), consolidation and residuum. Asking about the weeks before the first clear delusion often reveals trema.",
s="Jaspers, General Psychopathology; Conrad, Die beginnende Schizophrenie (1958)",
h="Wahnstimmung = “the WARNING mood”: the stage is set, the play hasn’t started."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx thought insertion vs obsession", m="ask", l=3, hard=True,
q="A patient reports unwanted thoughts of harming her baby. Which question best distinguishes obsessional intrusive thoughts from thought insertion?",
a="“When these thoughts come, do they feel like your own thoughts, even if you hate them, or like someone else’s thoughts put into your mind?”",
ae="In obsessions, the patient recognizes the thoughts as products of her own mind (ego-dystonic but owned). In thought insertion, the sense of ownership is lost: the thought is experienced as placed from outside. The question targets authorship, which is the core phenomenological difference.",
w=[("“How much do these thoughts distress you?”",
"Both obsessional and psychotic experiences can be distressing. Distress does not discriminate."),
("“Do you feel you have to do something to undo the thoughts?”",
"Neutralizing is typical of OCD, but patients with psychosis can develop rituals too. It is not the defining distinction."),
("“How often do you have these thoughts each day?”",
"Frequency does not separate the two phenomena.")],
x="Phenomenologists distinguish the sense of ownership (this is my thought) from the sense of agency (I am thinking it). In thought insertion, ownership in the sense of happening in my mind remains, but authorship is attributed elsewhere. This precision matters clinically: misdiagnosing perinatal obsessions as psychosis can lead to unnecessary separation of mother and infant and to antipsychotic exposure.",
s="Sims, Symptoms in the Mind; Gallagher on ownership vs agency",
h="OCD: “My thought, I hate it.” Insertion: “Not my thought, it was put in.” Ask whose name is on the thought."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx functional vs reflex hallucination", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="A patient hears voices only while the tap is running, and the voices stop when it is turned off. What is this called?",
a="A functional hallucination: a real stimulus in a modality triggers a hallucination in the same modality, both perceived together",
ae="In functional hallucinations, the triggering stimulus and the hallucination occur in the same sensory modality and are perceived simultaneously (voices in running water, in a fan, in traffic). The patient hears both the water and the voices.",
w=[("A reflex hallucination: a stimulus in one modality triggers a hallucination in another",
"Reflex hallucinations cross modalities, for example feeling a pain when hearing a sound. Here both stimulus and hallucination are auditory."),
("An illusion: the sound of water is misperceived as voices",
"In an illusion, the real stimulus is transformed into something else. In a functional hallucination, the real stimulus is perceived as itself and the hallucination occurs alongside it."),
("Extracampine hallucination: a voice heard outside the sensory field",
"Extracampine hallucinations are perceived beyond the possible range of the sense, such as a voice from another city.")],
x="These distinctions come from classic descriptive psychopathology (Sims, Fish). They are niche, but they show the precision expert interviewers bring: Ask “Do you hear the water and the voices at the same time, or does the water turn into voices?” This single question separates functional hallucination from illusion.",
s="Sims, Symptoms in the Mind; Fish’s Clinical Psychopathology",
h="Functional = “FUNnel in the same sense” (water + voice together). Reflex = “jumps senses.” Illusion = “water becomes voice.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx pseudohallucination", m="ask", l=3, hard=True,
q="In the Jaspers tradition, what distinguishes a pseudohallucination from a true hallucination?",
a="It lacks the full substantiality of a perception and is experienced in inner subjective space rather than outer objective space",
ae="Jaspers, following Kandinsky, described pseudohallucinations as figurative, not substantial, and located in inner space, like a vivid image that is not under voluntary control. True hallucinations have the quality of real perceptions in external space. Insight was not part of Jaspers’ definition, although later authors (Hare) used insight as the criterion, which caused confusion.",
w=[("The patient always recognizes it as unreal, while true hallucinations are never recognized",
"Insight-based definitions (Hare) differ from Jaspers. Many patients with psychosis do not consider their pseudohallucinations unreal, and some with true hallucinations do recognize them as unreal."),
("It is produced by organic disease, whereas true hallucinations are psychogenic",
"The distinction is phenomenological, not etiological."),
("It occurs only when the patient is falling asleep or waking",
"Hypnagogic and hypnopompic experiences are distinct phenomena, although some resemble pseudohallucinations.")],
x="Because definitions conflict, many experts suggest describing the experience precisely instead of using the term: location (inside or outside the head), vividness, voluntary control, recognition as real or unreal, and whether it is experienced as one’s own. The term pseudohallucination is often used, wrongly, to imply “not real” or “not psychotic,” which can lead to underestimating risk in personality disorders or trauma.",
s="Jaspers, General Psychopathology; Sims, Symptoms in the Mind",
h="Pseudo = “in the inner theater, not on the street.” Describe the seat, not just the show."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx ease ipseity", m="ask", l=3, hard=True,
q="The Examination of Anomalous Self-Experience (EASE) is a semi-structured interview. What is it designed to capture?",
a="Subtle, often pre-psychotic disturbances of basic self-experience, such as diminished sense of being a subject and hyperreflexivity",
ae="EASE (Parnas and colleagues, 2005) explores disturbances of ipseity, the minimal self: thought pressure, loss of thought ownership, diminished presence, hyperreflexivity, disturbances of demarcation, bodily estrangement, and existential reorientation. These are often present years before frank psychosis and are more frequent in the schizophrenia spectrum than in other disorders.",
w=[("First-rank symptoms in the acute phase of schizophrenia",
"First-rank symptoms are florid. EASE targets subtler self-disorders that can precede and accompany psychosis."),
("Personality traits measured on a five-factor scale",
"EASE is phenomenological and clinician-administered. It does not assess trait dimensions."),
("Dissociative identity states and amnesia",
"Dissociation is assessed by tools such as the SCID-D. EASE self-disorders differ phenomenologically from dissociation.")],
x="Sass and Parnas’ ipseity model of schizophrenia describes hyperreflexivity (exaggerated self-consciousness about normally tacit processes), diminished self-affection (weakened sense of existing as a subject) and disturbed grip on the world. Interviewing for them requires open, patient questioning and examples from the patient’s life, because patients rarely volunteer them and often lack words for them.",
s="Parnas et al., EASE, Psychopathology 2005; Sass & Parnas, Schizophr Bull 2003",
h="EASE finds “the crack in the I.” Ipseity = “I-ness.”"),

dict(d=D, c="ivx praecox feeling", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="Henricus Rümke described the “praecox feeling.” What was it, and what is its modern status?",
a="The interviewer’s intuitive sense of a lack of contact with the patient with schizophrenia; it is now studied as a phenomenological and diagnostic phenomenon, with debated reliability",
ae="Rümke (1941) described a characteristic feeling in the clinician when meeting a person with schizophrenia: a disturbance of the normal mutual attunement, an inability to make contact. It was once considered diagnostic. It is now debated: it may capture real intersubjective alterations, but it is vulnerable to bias and has variable reliability.",
w=[("A specific EEG pattern seen in early-onset schizophrenia",
"The praecox feeling is a subjective intersubjective experience in the clinician, not an EEG finding."),
("The patient’s sense of being prematurely aged",
"The name refers to dementia praecox, not to premature aging."),
("A validated rating scale for negative symptoms",
"It is not a rating scale, although some researchers have tried to operationalize it.")],
x="Minkowski described a related idea: loss of vital contact with reality. Recent phenomenological work suggests the praecox feeling arises from subtle disruptions of embodied interaction (timing, gaze, prosody). Use it as a hypothesis to examine, never as a diagnosis by itself, and check for alternative explanations including autism, negative symptoms from medication, cultural differences and your own bias.",
s="Rümke (1941); Minkowski, La Schizophrénie (1927)",
h="Praecox feeling: “the handshake that doesn’t land.” A clue, never a verdict."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx double bookkeeping", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="A patient insists he is the emperor of France, yet calmly queues for his medication and asks the nurse for coffee money. Which concept describes this?",
a="Double bookkeeping: delusional and ordinary realities coexist without the patient acting fully on the delusion",
ae="Bleuler described doppelte Buchführung: patients with schizophrenia often keep a delusional reality and a practical, shared reality side by side. Louis Sass has explored this as evidence that delusions are not just false beliefs but often a different mode of experience.",
w=[("Malingering, because a true delusion would always be acted upon",
"Many genuine delusions are not acted upon. Inconsistency between belief and action is common in real psychosis and does not prove malingering."),
("Folie à deux, in which a delusion is shared with another person",
"Folie à deux describes shared delusions between two people. It has nothing to do with this inconsistency."),
("Anosognosia, the lack of awareness of illness",
"Anosognosia concerns awareness of illness. Double bookkeeping concerns the coexistence of two realities.")],
x="Interview implications: ask whether the patient acts on the belief, how the two realities relate, and whether the patient experiences the delusion as literally true in the same way as ordinary facts. Risk assessment depends on this: delusions that are acted on (especially persecutory ones linked to anger) carry more risk.",
s="Bleuler, Dementia Praecox (1911); Sass, The Paradoxes of Delusion",
h="Double bookkeeping: “Emperor on paper, queuing in practice.” Two ledgers, one mind."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx form vs content", m="ask", l=2,
q="A patient says: “My neighbor sends me messages through the television.” Jaspers would ask the interviewer to separate form from content. What is the form here?",
a="The mode of experience, such as a delusional belief, a perception through the TV, or a thought heard aloud",
ae="Content is what the patient talks about (the neighbor, messages). Form is the type of mental phenomenon: is this a delusion of reference, an auditory hallucination from the TV, an illusion, or a culturally shaped belief? Content is shaped by culture and biography; form carries more diagnostic weight.",
w=[("The identity of the neighbor and the meaning of the messages",
"These are content. Content is crucial for formulation and risk but not for phenomenological classification."),
("The emotional reaction the patient has to the messages",
"Affect is important, but it is not what Jaspers meant by form."),
("The diagnosis the clinician assigns to the experience",
"Diagnosis is an inference from forms and course. Form is the description itself.")],
x="Jaspers’ distinction lets you recognize the same form across very different contents (a Lebanese patient and a Canadian patient may describe different persecutors but have identical delusions of reference). It also prevents overvaluing bizarre content: the strangeness of what is said is not what defines psychosis.",
s="Jaspers, General Psychopathology",
h="Form = the GLASS; content = the WINE. Diagnose the glass, understand the wine."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx clerambault mental automatism", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="In French psychopathology, Gaëtan Gatian de Clérambault described “petit automatisme mental.” What does the term refer to?",
a="Early, subtle, neutral phenomena such as thought echo, thoughts running ahead, or empty thinking, which precede and are not explained by delusional content",
ae="Clérambault described mental automatism as a primary, mechanical disturbance, initially neutral (anideic) and without emotional content: thought echo, anticipation of thoughts, enunciation of acts, emancipation of abstract ideas. The delusion, in his view, came later as an attempt to explain these intrusions. Lacan later called these “elementary phenomena,” a key concept in his diagnosis of psychosis.",
w=[("The catatonic automatic obedience seen in stupor",
"Automatic obedience is a motor sign of catatonia. Clérambault’s automatism concerns thought and language."),
("Repetitive habit behaviors in OCD",
"Compulsions are intentional and related to anxiety. Mental automatism is experienced as alien, mechanical and not one’s own."),
("Erotomania, which Clérambault also described",
"Clérambault is famous for describing erotomania (de Clérambault syndrome). That is a separate passionate delusion, not mental automatism.")],
x="The Lacanian diagnostic interview (présentation de malades, preliminary interviews) listens for these elementary phenomena: small ruptures in the chain of meaning, words with a strange weight, a sense of being spoken. This tradition offers a fine-grained approach to detecting psychosis that is not florid, sometimes called ordinary psychosis by Miller.",
s="Clérambault, Œuvres psychiatriques; Lacan, Seminar III (The Psychoses)",
h="Petit automatisme = “the motor running before the driver arrives.” Delusion is the driver’s later story."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx blankenburg natural self evidence", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="Wolfgang Blankenburg’s patient Anne Rau said she lacked something everyone else has, the ability to know “how to go about things.” What did Blankenburg call this disturbance?",
a="The loss of natural self-evidence: the tacit, taken-for-granted understanding of everyday life",
ae="Blankenburg (1971) described the loss of natural self-evidence (Verlust der natürlichen Selbstverständlichkeit) as a core of nonpsychotic schizophrenic states (symptom-poor schizophrenia). Everyday common sense becomes problematic and the person must think explicitly about what others take for granted.",
w=[("Alexithymia, the inability to identify and name emotions",
"Alexithymia concerns emotion, not the common-sense grasp of everyday life."),
("Executive dysfunction secondary to frontal lobe injury",
"Neuropsychological deficits may overlap, but Blankenburg’s description is phenomenological and specific to the schizophrenia spectrum."),
("Social anxiety disorder, in which the person fears judgment",
"Social anxiety involves fear. The loss of natural self-evidence involves a breakdown of tacit understanding, often with perplexity rather than fear.")],
x="This links to Sass’s hyperreflexivity: what is tacit becomes explicit and problematic. Interview probes include: “Do things that others do automatically require effort for you?” or “Do you sometimes feel you don’t understand the obvious rules of everyday life?” Patients often describe this vividly when asked, though they rarely volunteer it.",
s="Blankenburg, Der Verlust der natürlichen Selbstverständlichkeit (1971)",
h="Natural self-evidence = “the manual everyone else was born with.” Anne Rau lost the manual."),

dict(d=D, c="ivx tangentiality vs derailment andreasen", m="eye", l=3, hard=True,
q="In Andreasen’s Thought, Language and Communication (TLC) scale, what separates tangentiality from derailment?",
a="Tangentiality refers to replies to questions that are oblique or irrelevant; derailment is a slippage of ideas in spontaneous speech",
ae="Andreasen deliberately restricted tangentiality to answers that miss the point of a question from the start. Derailment (loose associations) is a pattern of spontaneous speech in which ideas slip off track onto unrelated or obliquely related ones.",
w=[("Tangentiality never returns to the point, whereas derailment always does",
"Returning to the point distinguishes circumstantiality from tangentiality, not tangentiality from derailment."),
("Tangentiality occurs within sentences; derailment occurs between sentences",
"Disruption within sentences is incoherence (word salad). Derailment occurs between clauses or sentences."),
("Tangentiality is seen only in mania; derailment only in schizophrenia",
"Both occur across psychoses, including mania and schizophrenia.")],
x="Other TLC items include poverty of speech, poverty of content of speech, pressure of speech, distractible speech, incoherence, illogicality, clanging, neologisms, word approximations, circumstantiality, loss of goal, perseveration, echolalia, blocking, stilted speech and self-reference. Precision lets you track change over time: poverty of content and loss of goal may persist as residual features even when florid derailment has resolved.",
s="Andreasen, Thought, Language and Communication scale, Arch Gen Psychiatry 1979",
h="Tangential = “answers the wrong question.” Derailed = “the train jumps tracks on its own.”"),
]
